%I A398377 #64 Nov 11 2026 10:11:52
%S A398377 5,67137387817,67137387847,67137387917,67138301689,67138301707,
%T A398377 67138301861,67138301873,67138301897,67138301921,67138302103,
%U A398377 67138302577,67138302613,67138302641,67138305961,67138305973,67138306061,67138306073,67138306097,67138306099,67138306127
%N A398377 Primes p for which pi_{24;7,11}(p) - pi_{24;1,5}(p) = -1, where pi_{m;a,b,...}(x) is the number of primes <= x which are congruent to a or b or ... (mod m).
%C A398377 From _Leandro Hector Gallego_ and _Jianing Song_: (Start)
%C A398377 This sequence is about one of the 21 prime races modulo 24 between cosets of index 4 in (Z/24Z)*; see A401958 for more details. Specifically, here we have H = {1,5} and S = {7,11}, corresponding to T = {chi_{-4}, chi_24}, where chi_d = (d/.) is the Kronecker symbol viewed as a character modulo N = 24.
%C A398377 This sequence gives primes p such that Sum_{primes q<=p} (chi_{-4}(q) + chi_24(q))/2 = 1.
%C A398377 Assuming GRH and LI (see A401958), the set of x with pi_{24;7,11}(x) > pi_{24;1,5}(x) has logarithmic density 0.99327, making the race between {1,5} and {7,11} the 12th most biased among the 21 races of cosets of index 4 in (Z/24Z)*; see the Zenodo preprint below.
%C A398377 Let D(x) = pi_{24;7,11}(x) - pi_{24;1,5}(x). Below x0 = 7*10^12 there are respectively 30659637567, 30659688364, 30659698140, 30659716342, 30659718072, 30659718286, 30659752652, and 30659759379 primes congruent to 1, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19, or 23 modulo 24, giving D(x0) = 88551. There are 3 sign-changing zones giving a total of 35309 terms below x0:
%C A398377   (1) a(1) = 5;
%C A398377   (2) a(2..16284) = 67137387817-67803193663 (there are 17475181 primes a(2) <= p <= a(16284) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -2669 at p = 67465004693);
%C A398377   (3) a(16285..35309) = 480101451701-480772481741 (there are 20589720 primes a(16285) <= p <= a(35309) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -1257 at p = 480403884817).
%C A398377 The total number of primes p <= x0 with D(p) < 0 is 38064902. (End)
%H A398377 Jianing Song, <a href="/A398377/b398377.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..35309</a> (all terms below 7*10^12)
%H A398377 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24020025">Logarithmic densities of prime number races between the cosets of a subgroup of index 4 modulo 24</a>, preprint, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A398377 Greg Martin and Daniel Fiorilli, <a href="https://personal.math.ubc.ca/~gerg/slides/Chennai-31Aug10.pdf">Prime number races: An asymptotic formula for the densities</a>, Analytic and Combinatorial Number Theory, Institute of Mathematical Sciences, Chennai, India. August 31, 2010.
%H A398377 Greg Martin, <a href="https://personal.math.ubc.ca/~gerg/slides/Canberra-29Nov18.pdf">Prime number races</a>, Pure Math Seminar, UNSW Canberra. November 29, 2018.
%H A398377 Andrey S. Shchebetov, <a href="https://math101.guru/wp-content/uploads/2018/09/01-A3-Presentation-v7.3EN-no.pdf">Testing Chebyshev's bias for prime numbers up to 10^15</a>, EUCYS 2018.
%H A398377 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chebyshev%27s_bias">Chebyshev's bias</a>.
%o A398377 (PARI) my(k=0); forprime(p=2, 1e11, if(p%24==1||p%24==5, k--); if(p%24==7||p%24==11, k++); if(k==-1, print1(p, ", ")))
%Y A398377 See A401958 for related sequences.
%K A398377 nonn,changed
%O A398377 1,1
%A A398377 _Jianing Song_, Oct 18 2026
